        "LESSON-SPECIFIC THINKING — Work through a small labelled example. Identify each input and unit, state assumptions, perform the calculation or comparison, then check the result independently. Retain source evidence and explain what the result does and does not prove. Go deeper by asking why the approach fits, what alternative you considered and what new evidence could change your answer. Explain your reasoning to a classmate who has not studied the lesson.",
        "COMMON ERRORS AND QUALITY CHECKS — Confusing sales with profit, overlooking fees, treating unpaid invoices as cash and investing heavily before testing demand. Check accuracy, relevance, safety, completeness and clarity before relying on your result. Inspect source information and calculations where relevant, respect privacy and applicable rules, and state uncertainties. Safety-critical work must use approved procedures and appropriately qualified supervision.",
        "PRACTICAL EXERCISE — Prepare a sample offer, estimate the costs, calculate the amount remaining before other expenses and record three fictional transactions. Record your goal, resources, steps, result, difficulty and one improvement for a second attempt. Use fictional or anonymised data whenever real names, customer records or private account details are unnecessary.",
