        "KEY TERMS — Audience: intended people; reach: people exposed to content; engagement: interactions; conversion: desired action completed; call to action: instruction for the next step; campaign: coordinated marketing activity. Build a glossary in your own notes. For each term, write a definition in your own words, an example and one related term it could be confused with. This helps you decide when a concept is appropriate and when it is not.",
        "HOW TO APPLY THE KNOWLEDGE — Set a measurable objective, define the audience, choose an appropriate channel, plan useful content, include a clear next action, publish responsibly and assess results over a defined period. Record important inputs, decisions and checks. Separate verified facts from assumptions. If information is missing, write down what still needs checking rather than silently guessing. Finally, compare the result with the original task.",
        "WORKED EXAMPLE — A local training provider publishes a course explainer with accurate fees and dates, links to its official enrolment page and tracks relevant enquiries rather than likes alone. Relate it to “Create a simple content plan”: identify the problem, name the concept being applied, explain the main step and state how you would verify the result. The example is a learning model; real situations can have different facts and constraints.",
        "LESSON-SPECIFIC THINKING — Connect the concept to a defined audience and measurable action. Check that the message is accurate and suitable for the channel. Track a result tied to the goal, compare it over a defined period and use evidence to make one improvement. Go deeper by asking why the approach fits, what alternative you considered and what new evidence could change your answer. Explain your reasoning to a classmate who has not studied the lesson.",
        "COMMON ERRORS AND QUALITY CHECKS — Fake engagement, unverified claims, unsuitable audiences, posting customer images without permission and ignoring privacy requirements. Check accuracy, relevance, safety, completeness and clarity before relying on your result. Inspect source information and calculations where relevant, respect privacy and applicable rules, and state uncertainties. Safety-critical work must use approved procedures and appropriately qualified supervision.",
        "PRACTICAL EXERCISE — Draft a one-week content plan with audience, message, format, call to action and one meaningful success measure. Record your goal, resources, steps, result, difficulty and one improvement for a second attempt. Use fictional or anonymised data whenever real names, customer records or private account details are unnecessary.",
        "SELF-ASSESSMENT — Without looking at the notes, answer: (1) What is the main idea in Create a simple content plan? (2) Which two terms matter most? (3) How would you apply the idea in Digital Marketing & Social Media? (4) What mistake should you avoid? (5) What evidence would show that your result is correct? Check your answers against the guide and correct any gaps.",
        "EXTEND YOUR LEARNING — Find one further example, case, dataset or practice task related to Create a simple content plan. Assess who produced it, why it is relevant and what evidence supports it. Compare it with your own exercise, write one new insight and record a question you want to investigate next."
