        "LEARNING OUTCOMES — By the end of this lesson in Computer Applications & Office Skills, you should be able to explain the topic in your own words, recognise when it is useful, apply it to a realistic task and check whether the result is reliable. You should also be able to describe one limitation instead of assuming that one method works in every situation.",
        "DETAILED EXPLANATION — Office software helps people create documents, presentations, tables and structured records. Useful work depends on choosing the correct tool, organising information, applying readable formatting and checking accuracy. A good file should be understandable to another person and easy to locate later. Connect this explanation to “Manage files and folders”: ask what the concept helps you understand or decide, what information it depends on and how the outcome might change if an important condition changed. Define unfamiliar terms and add a small example so that understanding goes beyond memorising words.",
        "KEY TERMS — Document: formatted text; worksheet: grid of spreadsheet cells; formula: expression that calculates a result; template: reusable structure; file path: saved location; version: a particular saved state. Build a glossary in your own notes. For each term, write a definition in your own words, an example and one related term it could be confused with. This helps you decide when a concept is appropriate and when it is not.",
        "HOW TO APPLY THE KNOWLEDGE — Identify the audience and purpose, structure the information, apply consistent headings or columns, check formulas and spelling, save with a descriptive name and reopen the file. Record important inputs, decisions and checks. Separate verified facts from assumptions. If information is missing, write down what still needs checking rather than silently guessing. Finally, compare the result with the original task.",
        "WORKED EXAMPLE — A business prepares a quotation in a document and an inventory list in a spreadsheet. It checks customer details, quantities and formula totals before sending the quotation. Relate it to “Manage files and folders”: identify the problem, name the concept being applied, explain the main step and state how you would verify the result. The example is a learning model; real situations can have different facts and constraints.",
        "LESSON-SPECIFIC THINKING — Ask what you should be able to explain or do after studying Manage files and folders. Write an answer, demonstrate it with an example, check it against reliable information and identify what additional practice would help you work independently. Go deeper by asking why the approach fits, what alternative you considered and what new evidence could change your answer. Explain your reasoning to a classmate who has not studied the lesson.",
        "COMMON ERRORS AND QUALITY CHECKS — Overwriting the only copy, unchecked formulas, inconsistent formats, inaccessible layouts and unnecessary disclosure of records. Check accuracy, relevance, safety, completeness and clarity before relying on your result. Inspect source information and calculations where relevant, respect privacy and applicable rules, and state uncertainties. Safety-critical work must use approved procedures and appropriately qualified supervision.",
        "PRACTICAL EXERCISE — Create a one-page document and a small spreadsheet, check both for errors and reopen them from their saved location. Record your goal, resources, steps, result, difficulty and one improvement for a second attempt. Use fictional or anonymised data whenever real names, customer records or private account details are unnecessary.",
