            "Keep stock and prices updated. Avoid misleading claims, copied images used without permission and promises that cannot be fulfilled.",
            "Practice: draft a product listing that includes a description, price, delivery information and return terms.",
            "DETAILED STUDY GUIDE v3 — Setting up a trustworthy online product listing. Use this guide alongside the original lesson notes.",
            "LEARNING OUTCOMES — By the end of this lesson in E-commerce & Online Selling, you should be able to explain the topic in your own words, recognise when it is useful, apply it to a realistic task and check whether the result is reliable. You should also be able to describe one limitation instead of assuming that one method works in every situation.",
            "DETAILED EXPLANATION — E-commerce allows customers to discover, order and pay for goods or services online. Trust depends on accurate listings, transparent prices and conditions, dependable fulfilment, verified payments and responsive communication. The order record should match what is actually delivered. Connect this explanation to “Setting up a trustworthy online product listing”: ask what the concept helps you understand or decide, what information it depends on and how the outcome might change if an important condition changed. Define unfamiliar terms and add a small example so that understanding goes beyond memorising words.",
            "KEY TERMS — Listing: product information for buyers; fulfilment: preparing and delivering an order; payment verification: checking the official transaction record; return policy: conditions for returns; inventory: available stock. Build a glossary in your own notes. For each term, write a definition in your own words, an example and one related term it could be confused with. This helps you decide when a concept is appropriate and when it is not.",
            "HOW TO APPLY THE KNOWLEDGE — Describe the product accurately, state the price and conditions, confirm stock, verify payments in official records, arrange delivery and keep appropriate order records. Record important inputs, decisions and checks. Separate verified facts from assumptions. If information is missing, write down what still needs checking rather than silently guessing. Finally, compare the result with the original task.",
            "WORKED EXAMPLE — A seller lists a used laptop, states its real condition and specifications, and confirms payment through the payment provider rather than trusting a screenshot. Relate it to “Setting up a trustworthy online product listing”: identify the problem, name the concept being applied, explain the main step and state how you would verify the result. The example is a learning model; real situations can have different facts and constraints.",
            "LESSON-SPECIFIC THINKING — Consider both sides of the transaction: what the customer expects, what was promised, what records the agreement and how completion is verified. State scope, timing and conditions clearly and protect personal data. Go deeper by asking why the approach fits, what alternative you considered and what new evidence could change your answer. Explain your reasoning to a classmate who has not studied the lesson.",
            "COMMON ERRORS AND QUALITY CHECKS — Misrepresenting products, fake payment messages, unclear return conditions and careless handling of customer data. Check accuracy, relevance, safety, completeness and clarity before relying on your result. Inspect source information and calculations where relevant, respect privacy and applicable rules, and state uncertainties. Safety-critical work must use approved procedures and appropriately qualified supervision.",
            "PRACTICAL EXERCISE — Draft a product listing and an order checklist covering stock, payment confirmation, delivery and after-sales support. Record your goal, resources, steps, result, difficulty and one improvement for a second attempt. Use fictional or anonymised data whenever real names, customer records or private account details are unnecessary.",
            "SELF-ASSESSMENT — Without looking at the notes, answer: (1) What is the main idea in Setting up a trustworthy online product listing? (2) Which two terms matter most? (3) How would you apply the idea in E-commerce & Online Selling? (4) What mistake should you avoid? (5) What evidence would show that your result is correct? Check your answers against the guide and correct any gaps.",
            "EXTEND YOUR LEARNING — Find one further example, case, dataset or practice task related to Setting up a trustworthy online product listing. Assess who produced it, why it is relevant and what evidence supports it. Compare it with your own exercise, write one new insight and record a question you want to investigate next."
