            "DETAILED EXPLANATION — Freelancing means delivering agreed work to clients, often independently. Sustainable freelancing combines a demonstrable skill with clear scope, realistic pricing, reliable communication and delivery discipline. A portfolio shows ability; a written agreement reduces misunderstandings about payment and revisions. Connect this explanation to “Proposals, pricing and client agreements”: ask what the concept helps you understand or decide, what information it depends on and how the outcome might change if an important condition changed. Define unfamiliar terms and add a small example so that understanding goes beyond memorising words.",
            "KEY TERMS — Portfolio: work samples; proposal: offer outlining scope and value; deliverable: promised result; scope: agreed work boundaries; revision: requested change; invoice: request for payment. Build a glossary in your own notes. For each term, write a definition in your own words, an example and one related term it could be confused with. This helps you decide when a concept is appropriate and when it is not.",
            "HOW TO APPLY THE KNOWLEDGE — Choose a service you can deliver, prepare honest samples, identify legitimate prospects, define deliverables and deadlines, agree a price, document the arrangement and track payment. Record important inputs, decisions and checks. Separate verified facts from assumptions. If information is missing, write down what still needs checking rather than silently guessing. Finally, compare the result with the original task.",
            "WORKED EXAMPLE — A designer offers three graphics with two revision rounds and a delivery date. The proposal states the fee, file formats and what counts as extra work. Relate it to “Proposals, pricing and client agreements”: identify the problem, name the concept being applied, explain the main step and state how you would verify the result. The example is a learning model; real situations can have different facts and constraints.",
            "LESSON-SPECIFIC THINKING — Consider both sides of the transaction: what the customer expects, what was promised, what records the agreement and how completion is verified. State scope, timing and conditions clearly and protect personal data. Go deeper by asking why the approach fits, what alternative you considered and what new evidence could change your answer. Explain your reasoning to a classmate who has not studied the lesson.",
            "COMMON ERRORS AND QUALITY CHECKS — Pay-to-get-a-job scams, vague scope, unpaid extra work and sharing sensitive information with unverified people. Check accuracy, relevance, safety, completeness and clarity before relying on your result. Inspect source information and calculations where relevant, respect privacy and applicable rules, and state uncertainties. Safety-critical work must use approved procedures and appropriately qualified supervision.",
            "PRACTICAL EXERCISE — Draft a service proposal with deliverables, timeframe, price basis, revisions, client responsibilities and safety checks. Record your goal, resources, steps, result, difficulty and one improvement for a second attempt. Use fictional or anonymised data whenever real names, customer records or private account details are unnecessary.",
            "SELF-ASSESSMENT — Without looking at the notes, answer: (1) What is the main idea in Proposals, pricing and client agreements? (2) Which two terms matter most? (3) How would you apply the idea in Freelancing & Digital Careers? (4) What mistake should you avoid? (5) What evidence would show that your result is correct? Check your answers against the guide and correct any gaps.",
