    "content": [
      "A budget compares expected income with planned spending. Separate essential expenses, optional spending and savings where possible. Total each category and compare the planned amount with the money available before committing to a purchase.",
      "Unit price helps compare products sold in different package sizes. Divide the price by the quantity to find the cost per item, kilogram or litre. The cheapest package is not always the best choice if quality, expiry dates, transport or waste differ.",
      "Change is the amount returned after a payment. Subtract the total cost from the amount paid and check that the result is sensible. Keep receipts and written records when tracking business or household spending.",
      "Practice: compare a 2 kg bag of flour costing KES 300 with a 5 kg bag costing KES 700. Find each price per kilogram, decide which is cheaper per kilogram, and identify one other factor to consider.",
      "DETAILED STUDY GUIDE v3 — Money, budgets and unit prices. Use this guide alongside the original lesson notes.",
      "LEARNING OUTCOMES — By the end of this lesson in Basic Numeracy & Mathematics, you should be able to explain the topic in your own words, recognise when it is useful, apply it to a realistic task and check whether the result is reliable. You should also be able to describe one limitation instead of assuming that one method works in every situation.",
      "DETAILED EXPLANATION — Numeracy applies numbers and quantities to everyday decisions. Place value, operations, fractions, decimals, percentages, measurement and estimation build on one another. A correct calculation also needs correct units and a result that makes sense in context. Connect this explanation to “Money, budgets and unit prices”: ask what the concept helps you understand or decide, what information it depends on and how the outcome might change if an important condition changed. Define unfamiliar terms and add a small example so that understanding goes beyond memorising words.",
      "KEY TERMS — Place value: value determined by position; estimate: approximate answer; fraction: part of a whole; percentage: amount per hundred; unit price: cost for one unit; measurement: assigning a number and unit to a quantity. Build a glossary in your own notes. For each term, write a definition in your own words, an example and one related term it could be confused with. This helps you decide when a concept is appropriate and when it is not.",
      "HOW TO APPLY THE KNOWLEDGE — Read the question, identify known values and the unknown, choose an operation, write units, estimate a likely range, calculate and check the answer another way when possible. Record important inputs, decisions and checks. Separate verified facts from assumptions. If information is missing, write down what still needs checking rather than silently guessing. Finally, compare the result with the original task.",
      "WORKED EXAMPLE — Four equal notebooks cost KES 200. Unit price is 200 divided by 4, or KES 50 each. An estimate helps detect a misplaced digit. Relate it to “Money, budgets and unit prices”: identify the problem, name the concept being applied, explain the main step and state how you would verify the result. The example is a learning model; real situations can have different facts and constraints.",
      "LESSON-SPECIFIC THINKING — Work through a small labelled example. Identify each input and unit, state assumptions, perform the calculation or comparison, then check the result independently. Retain source evidence and explain what the result does and does not prove. Go deeper by asking why the approach fits, what alternative you considered and what new evidence could change your answer. Explain your reasoning to a classmate who has not studied the lesson.",
      "COMMON ERRORS AND QUALITY CHECKS — Misreading the question, mixing units, applying a percentage incorrectly and accepting an unreasonable result. Check accuracy, relevance, safety, completeness and clarity before relying on your result. Inspect source information and calculations where relevant, respect privacy and applicable rules, and state uncertainties. Safety-critical work must use approved procedures and appropriately qualified supervision.",
      "PRACTICAL EXERCISE — Create three everyday problems involving money, quantities and measurements; show calculations and check each answer. Record your goal, resources, steps, result, difficulty and one improvement for a second attempt. Use fictional or anonymised data whenever real names, customer records or private account details are unnecessary.",
      "SELF-ASSESSMENT — Without looking at the notes, answer: (1) What is the main idea in Money, budgets and unit prices? (2) Which two terms matter most? (3) How would you apply the idea in Basic Numeracy & Mathematics? (4) What mistake should you avoid? (5) What evidence would show that your result is correct? Check your answers against the guide and correct any gaps.",
      "EXTEND YOUR LEARNING — Find one further example, case, dataset or practice task related to Money, budgets and unit prices. Assess who produced it, why it is relevant and what evidence supports it. Compare it with your own exercise, write one new insight and record a question you want to investigate next."
    ]
