      "LESSON-SPECIFIC THINKING — Ask what you should be able to explain or do after studying Maps, direction and local geography. Write an answer, demonstrate it with an example, check it against reliable information and identify what additional practice would help you work independently. Go deeper by asking why the approach fits, what alternative you considered and what new evidence could change your answer. Explain your reasoning to a classmate who has not studied the lesson.",
      "COMMON ERRORS AND QUALITY CHECKS — Stereotyping, sharing unverified claims, ignoring viewpoints and presenting personal belief as a confirmed rule. Check accuracy, relevance, safety, completeness and clarity before relying on your result. Inspect source information and calculations where relevant, respect privacy and applicable rules, and state uncertainties. Safety-critical work must use approved procedures and appropriately qualified supervision.",
