            "DETAILED EXPLANATION — Social studies explores how people, places, resources, institutions and histories shape community life. Citizenship includes rights and responsibilities. Understanding public issues requires listening to different perspectives, checking information and considering effects on others. Connect this explanation to “Rights, responsibilities and citizenship”: ask what the concept helps you understand or decide, what information it depends on and how the outcome might change if an important condition changed. Define unfamiliar terms and add a small example so that understanding goes beyond memorising words.",
            "KEY TERMS — Community: people connected by place or interests; map key: symbols explained on a map; direction: position relative to a reference; right: protected entitlement; responsibility: duty; stakeholder: person or group affected. Build a glossary in your own notes. For each term, write a definition in your own words, an example and one related term it could be confused with. This helps you decide when a concept is appropriate and when it is not.",
            "HOW TO APPLY THE KNOWLEDGE — Define the place or issue, identify affected groups, distinguish facts from viewpoints, consult reliable sources and propose a respectful action. Record important inputs, decisions and checks. Separate verified facts from assumptions. If information is missing, write down what still needs checking rather than silently guessing. Finally, compare the result with the original task.",
